  "dimensions": "43.7 × 33.6 cm",
  "production_date": "after 1609",
  "brief_description": "Christ's body has been taken down from the Cross and is being carried on a shroud to his place of burial. His head rests limply on his shoulder; his pale, foreshortened body is the focus of a whole retinue of grieving figures, each expressing their sorrow in a different way.\n\nThis small picture is painted on copper, and the artist has exploited the material's reflective properties by painting a night scene, contrasting the darkness of the cave with the flickering light provided by the flaming torch falling on the figures.\n\nThis picture was once attributed to Ludovico Carracci but is now thought to be by Sisto Badalocchio, and is closely related to a design by Annibale Carracci.",
  "accession_date": "1837-01-01",
